# Diagnosing Battery Sag: Why Voltage Drops When Headlights Engage

When you turn on your headlights and see the battery terminal voltage dip, you are witnessing a direct measurement of the electrical load against the battery's internal impedance. In the scenario described, a fully charged battery starts at an unknown baseline (fresh from a charger), drops to 12.02V under headlight load, and recovers to 12.8V upon release. Is this behavior normal for a new battery? The short answer is yes, but understanding the magnitude of that drop requires looking past simple voltage readings into the physics of internal resistance and current draw.

To interpret these numbers accurately, we must first establish a stable baseline. A "fully charged" lead-acid battery at rest typically sits between 12.6V and 12.8V. If your initial reading was immediately after charging, you might be looking at surface charge levels that are slightly elevated compared to the chemical equilibrium of the cells. However, assuming a standard resting voltage of roughly 12.6V, a drop to 12.02V represents a sag of approximately 0.6V.

The source material provided offers a critical benchmark from *Testing Automotive Batteries and Starters*. It details a new battery rated at 650 CCA (Cold Cranking Amps) that exhibited an initial voltage of 12.51V. When subjected to a substantial 50-amp draw, the voltage dropped to 12.15Vâa sag of roughly 0.36V. This test highlights two vital concepts: current magnitude matters, and internal impedance is the limiting factor.

Halogen headlights (including high beams) typically consume around 55W each at 12V, totaling roughly 5-6 amps per bulb pair. If you run four bulbs (two low, two high), the draw is approximately 20-25 amps. Using Ohm's Law ($V = I \times R$), we can estimate the voltage sag based on internal resistance. The example battery had a plate impedance of 3.82 milliohms.

$$ \text{Sag} = 25\,\text{A} \times 0.00382\,\Omega \approx 0.095\,\text{V} $$

However, the observed drop in your test was significantly larger (~0.6V). Why? The primary culprit is likely the state of charge at the moment of testing. If the battery was just disconnected from a charger, it may not have reached true chemical equilibrium. Furthermore, real-world batteries often exhibit higher effective internal resistance due to surface films or slight stratification that hasn't yet evened out.

A sag of 0.6V under ~25A of load is generally acceptable for a new battery, provided the voltage recovers quickly after the load is removed. In your case, the recovery from 12.02V back to 12.8V indicates that the electrolyte is active and the plates are healthy. If the voltage had remained low or dropped further (below 11.5V) upon switching off due to inductive kickback from the alternator diodes, that would be a different diagnostic issue.

If you are building a custom automotive system or integrating battery monitoring into a larger project, measuring this sag can serve as a quick health check without needing complex impedance analyzers. You can implement a simple voltage logger using an Arduino or ESP32 that samples the battery voltage every 10ms when a load is toggled.

```cpp
// Pseudocode for logging voltage sag
float baseline_voltage = readBatteryVoltage(); // Wait 5 seconds for stabilization
bool load_active = false;

void loop() {
    if (load_active) {
        float current_sag = readBatteryVoltage();
        Serial.print("Load Voltage: ");
        Serial.println(current_sag, 2);

        // Check for excessive sag
        if (baseline_voltage - current_sag > 0.5) {
            Serial.println("WARNING: High Internal Resistance Detected");
        }
    } else {
        // Monitor recovery rate
        float post_load_voltage = readBatteryVoltage();
        if (post_load_voltage < 12.4 && time_since_load_off < 2000ms) {
             Serial.println("WARNING: Slow Recovery / Weak Battery");
        }
    }
}
```

The trade-off here is between diagnostic accuracy and system complexity. While a dedicated load tester provides precise CCA ratings, observing the natural voltage sag under known loads (like headlights) offers a practical, non-intrusive method to verify battery health in the field. Just ensure your measurement point is directly at the battery terminals; voltage drops across long gauge cables or corroded connectors can skew these readings significantly, leading to false positives regarding internal resistance.

Ultimately, your observation of a 12.02V dip under headlight load followed by a rapid recovery to 12.8V aligns with expected behavior for a healthy new battery, even if the exact theoretical sag calculation suggests a smaller drop. The discrepancy often stems from initial charging conditions and real-world impedance variations that textbook formulas don't always capture.
